  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "short": "It’s slow",
      "title": "“Checkout is slow.” Where?",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "Support tickets pile up: checkout takes several seconds. A checkout touches the gateway, orders, inventory, payments, a database and a third-party fraud API.\n\nYou can see a request waiting somewhere in this diagram — but the team can’t. Every server’s CPU looks fine, and nobody knows where to start.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "warning",
        "text": "If users find problems before you do, you are flying blind."
      },
      "changes": [],
      "metrics": {
        "detect": "2 h (support tickets)",
        "p99": "2.4 s",
        "signals": "none"
      },
      "overrides": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "short": "Metrics",
      "title": "Metrics: numbers over time",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Every service records a few numbers about the requests it handles — the [[RED]] method: **R**ate (requests/s), **E**rrors (failed share) and **D**uration (latency percentiles) — and sends them to a time-series store with dashboards.\n\nNow the picture is clear: inventory and the database are fast; the slowness lives on the payments path.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Common stacks: Prometheus + Grafana, Datadog, CloudWatch. Measure p99, not just averages. RED describes requests; for machines, watch **USE**: utilization, saturation, errors."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ RED metrics on every service",
        "+ Dashboard: p99 per service"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "detect": "5 min",
        "locate": "payments path",
        "p99": "2.4 s",
        "signals": "metrics"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "db": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "short": "Alerts",
      "title": "Alert on what users feel",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Don’t page people for “CPU above 80%” — it fires constantly and often means nothing. Alert on **symptoms** users feel.\n\nPick an [[SLI]] (say, the share of checkouts faster than 1 s), set an [[SLO]] target (99.9%), and alert when you’re burning through the remaining [[error budget]] too fast.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "An error budget turns reliability into a shared number: while it lasts, ship features; when it’s gone, fix reliability first."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ SLO: 99.9% of checkouts under 1 s",
        "+ Alert on error-budget burn"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "detect": "2 min",
        "locate": "payments path",
        "p99": "2.4 s",
        "signals": "metrics + alerts"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "db": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "short": "Logs",
      "title": "Logs: the story, line by line",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "body": "Metrics say *where*; [[logs]] say *what happened*. Each service writes timestamped events — but at thousands of requests a second, lines from different requests interleave into noise.\n\nThe trick: give every request an id at the gateway, pass it along, and include it in every log line. Filter by one id and you get one request’s story.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Log structured JSON, not free text — so you can filter by request id, user, service or status."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Request id on every log line"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "detect": "2 min",
        "locate": "payments → fraud API?",
        "p99": "2.4 s",
        "signals": "metrics + logs"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "db": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "short": "Traces",
      "title": "Traces: follow one request everywhere",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "A [[distributed trace]] records every hop of one request as a **span** with a start time and duration, all joined by the same trace id. Drawn as a waterfall, it shows exactly where the time goes.\n\nFollow the violet request: 2.1 of its 2.4 seconds are spent waiting for the third-party fraud API.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Tools: OpenTelemetry to collect, Jaeger, Tempo, Honeycomb or Datadog to explore. Instrument once, use everywhere."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Trace id propagated across services",
        "~ Cause found: fraud API ≈ 88% of the time"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "detect": "2 min",
        "locate": "5 min",
        "p99": "2.4 s",
        "signals": "metrics + logs + traces"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "db": {
          "emphasis": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s6",
      "short": "Fixed",
      "title": "Fix it, then prove it",
      "chapter": "solution",
      "body": "With the cause known, the fix is quick: call the fraud API with a 300 ms timeout, cache recent scores, and review the rare slow cases afterwards.\n\nThe same dashboards prove it worked: p99 drops from 2.4 s to 310 ms, and the SLO alert clears. Total time from page to cause: minutes, not hours.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Metrics tell you something is wrong, traces show where, logs explain why. You need all three."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ 300 ms timeout + cache on fraud checks",
        "~ Checkout p99 2.4 s → 310 ms"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "detect": "2 min",
        "locate": "5 min",
        "p99": "310 ms",
        "signals": "metrics + logs + traces"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "pay-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "op-3": {
          "visible": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s7",
      "short": "Incident",
      "title": "Case: 3 a.m. — errors spike",
      "chapter": "problem",
      "body": "The pager fires: checkout errors jumped from 0.1% to 6% at 03:12. Inventory is the culprit on the dashboard. The deploy log shows a config change to inventory at 03:10.\n\nGood practice is to **mitigate first**: roll the change back and restore service, and investigate the root cause afterwards — not the other way round.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Put deploys and config changes on your dashboards as markers. “What changed?” is the first question in almost every incident."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Config change to inventory at 03:10",
        "- Checkout errors 0.1% → 6%",
        "~ Rolled back at 03:16"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "detect": "1 min",
        "locate": "4 min",
        "p99": "900 ms",
        "signals": "metrics + logs + traces"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "gw-6": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "inv-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "pay-3": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "ug-6": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "go-6": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "oi-3": {
          "visible": false
        },
        "op-3": {
          "emphasis": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s8",
      "short": "Post-mortem",
      "title": "Learn from it: the post-mortem",
      "chapter": "insight",
      "body": "After the incident, the team writes a [[post-mortem]]: a timeline, the impact, the root cause, and the actions that stop it happening again.\n\nIt’s **blameless** — the question is how the system let a mistake through, not who made it. Here: config changes now go through the same canary rollout as code.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Each incident is expensive training data. Teams that write good post-mortems make the same mistake much less often."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ Post-mortem written",
        "+ Action: canary rollout for config too"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "detect": "1 min",
        "locate": "4 min",
        "p99": "310 ms",
        "signals": "metrics + logs + traces"
      },
      "overrides": {
        "gw-6": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "inv-3": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "ug-6": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "go-6": {
          "emphasis": true
        },
        "oi-3": {
          "emphasis": true
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s9",
      "short": "Recap",
      "title": "Recap",
      "chapter": "recap",
      "body": "• **Metrics** (rate, errors, duration) show where it hurts.\n• **Alert on SLOs** — what users feel — not on raw CPU.\n• **Logs** with a request id tell one request’s story.\n• **Traces** follow a request across every service.\n• In an incident, **mitigate first**; then write a **blameless post-mortem**.\n\nInstrument before you need it — the outage is the wrong time to add logging.",
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "That completes Reliability. Next module: **Architecture** — starting with monoliths vs microservices."
      },
